      "arabicLemma": "قوله والمنطق آلة قانونية",
      "english": "On “logic is a rule-governed instrument”: an instrument is an intermediary between an agent and the thing affected by the agent, through which the agent's effect reaches its recipient—like the pen between the writer and what is written. The word qānūn is said to come from Greek or Syriac, originally used for a ruling line in writing. In technical usage a canon is a universal proposition from which rulings concerning its subject's particular cases are learned. The term will be discussed in greater detail later. Thinking is the arrangement of known matters to obtain matters not yet known.\n\nLogic is called an *instrument* because it mediates between the rational power and the theoretical objects it seeks to acquire. It is called *rule-governed* because its problems formulate universal rules by which judgments concerning particular instances become known. For example, if one knows the rule that a universal affirmative converts to a particular affirmative, one can infer from “Every human is an animal” that “Some animal is human.” This example presupposes the intended existential context; it does not automatically settle the modern empty-domain interpretation of a universal proposition.\n\nThe definition ascribes protection from error to the *observance* of logical rules. Possessing the discipline itself does not confer infallibility: otherwise anyone trained in logic would never err, whereas one can plainly make mistakes by neglecting the art and failing to apply its precepts.\n\nThe definition's elements can now be distinguished. “Instrument” functions like a genus; “rule-governed” like a more remote differentia, excluding the particular tools used by craftsmen; and its final qualification like a nearer differentia, distinguishing logic from other rule-governed studies that preserve correctness in speech rather than in thinking—for instance grammar and morphology.\n\nStrictly, however, the definition is a *rasm* (descriptive definition), not a *ḥadd* (essential definition). An essential definition uses intrinsic constituents. Logic's instrumentality is relational to other disciplines, not something constituting its essence considered in isolation. Error-prevention is also its end, which is extrinsic to the thing defined; defining a science through its purpose is therefore descriptive.\n\nAn objection is that logic can be instrumental in relation to logic itself: one logical proposition is used to establish another. The initial answer says that such use occurs between distinct parts, rather than the entire science being instrumental to itself; to make instrumentality essential to the whole, one would need each problem to be instrumental to itself. The glossator suggests that the reply is more elaborate than necessary. Even if logic has some reflexive uses, its instrumental relation to another discipline is enough to show that the relationship is not intrinsic to logic as such. An essential attribute must belong to a thing through what it is, not merely through comparison with something else.",
