      "english": "On “according to the rule or method of Islam”: this qualification distinguishes kalām from philosophical inquiry, since philosophers also investigate the conditions of the first principle and the return, yet do not formally require their conclusions to conform to the religious law of Islam as a condition of the inquiry.\n\nThe intended exclusion should not be exaggerated. The point is that philosophical investigation does not *make conformity to the revealed law a formal criterion*, not that it positively requires its findings to *contradict* that law. Were one to assume the latter, a philosopher who accepts philosophy would, simply by that fact, be obliged to adopt positions contrary to the purified religious law and thus be judged an unbeliever; that does not follow from the methodological distinction. **Not taking a property into account is logically different from taking its absence into account.** The negative qualification on the criterion is not a positive criterion of nonconformity.",
