      "english": "The preposition min is understood as explicating a genus or class (bayān al-jins). It occurs especially often after the indefinite expressions mā and mahmā because of their breadth and ambiguity, as in Qurʾānic constructions equivalent to “whatever verse We abrogate” and “whatever sign you bring us.” Yet it can also follow other words, as in the Qurʾānic prohibition concerning the abomination of idols.\n\nThe prepositional phrase functions as a circumstantial adjunct (ḥāl) and is therefore in the accusative position in syntactic analysis. One can reconstruct it with an understood “being from the establishment of the doctrines of Islam.” The same applies to analogous constructions.\n\nCertain grammarians reject the category of explanatory min altogether. On their approach, this particular min is construed partitively instead. The difference between a genus-explaining and a partitive reading remains an interpretive alternative rather than one settled by the mere appearance of the preposition.",
